import{_ as o,o as t,c as s,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BN3Eol-0.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"UXD-019 — Select = focus","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"uxs/decisions/UXD-019-select-equals-focus.md","filePath":"uxs/decisions/UXD-019-select-equals-focus.md"}'),n={name:"uxs/decisions/UXD-019-select-equals-focus.md"};function i(r,e,l,d,c,h){return t(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="uxd-019-—-select-focus" tabindex="-1">UXD-019 — Select = focus <a class="header-anchor" href="#uxd-019-—-select-focus" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;UXD-019 — Select = focus&quot;">​</a></h1><ul><li><strong>Status</strong>: accepted</li><li><strong>Date</strong>: 2026-11-12 (proposed and decided)</li><li><strong>Decider</strong>: the operator, 2026-11-12 — every open question of <code>docs/wip/2026-11-12-slice-C1-brief.md</code> §11 approved as recommended</li><li><strong>Answers</strong>: E3, E5, SS4, I4, EP1, IM5, IM6 of the UX feedback plan; slice C1; deferred item L35</li><li><strong>Supersedes</strong>: the &quot;no camera move&quot; line of <a href="./UXD-017-one-index-on-every-scale">UXD-017</a> (Decision, Rows) and its &quot;a camera move on select&quot; deferral</li><li><strong>Changes</strong>: <code>src/lib/explore/body-ref.ts</code> (new, with its test), <code>src/lib/three/panel-framing.ts</code> (new, with its test), <code>src/lib/three/explore-scene-host.ts</code> (<code>focusBody</code>, <code>focusMember</code>, four outer Resets, the ring for every kind, the chip table, panel-aware framing, milestone picking), <code>src/lib/three/explore-solar-scene.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/three/iconic-trajectory.ts</code> (<code>pickTargets</code>, <code>PICK_KINDS</code>), <code>src/routes/explore/+page.svelte</code> (<code>selectBody</code>, <code>openBody</code>, <code>showKind</code>, <code>clearSelectionIfHidden</code>), <code>src/lib/explore/explore-url-sync.svelte.ts</code> (<code>?context=&amp;pick=</code>), <code>src/lib/missions-linkify.ts</code> (<code>sortNewestFirst</code>, link kinds), the five body panels, <code>BeltPanel</code>, <code>MissionPanel</code> (the message section, the action row&#39;s reserved height, <code>onBack</code>), <code>Panel.svelte</code> (<code>covered</code>, <code>onBack</code>), <code>src/lib/plan/destinations.ts</code> (new), <code>SmallBodyPanel</code> (Plan mission), <code>MissionMessageSection.svelte</code> (new), <code>MessagePanel.svelte</code> (deleted), <code>SurfaceScene.svelte</code> (ORBITERS, TRAVERSES), <code>HudControls.svelte</code>, <code>src/lib/universe/milky-way-visual.ts</code> (Cygnus X-1&#39;s pin), messages (<code>explore_layer_tip_dwarfs</code> reworded in 14 locales, 2 keys removed); <a href="./../UXS-014">UXS-014</a>, <a href="./../IA">IA</a>, <code>docs/adr/TA.md</code>, <code>docs/accessibility.md</code>, AGENTS.md, <code>src/routes/explore/README.md</code>, <code>src/lib/explore/README.md</code></li></ul><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>&quot;Selected&quot; is three things: the panel, the ring, the camera. Planets had all three, moons two, small bodies and belts one, the Sun one and a home view. Six ladders chose a body (canvas, INDEX row, &quot;Who lives here&quot;, <code>?id=</code>, the Belt members, mission links) and disagreed on Pluto and on what was a link. A chip hid a body without clearing its selection, 16 bodies rode the wrong chip in 3D and none in 2D, and four residents of the ruler were buttons that did nothing. Evidence: the brief&#39;s §2 and §3.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Selecting a body focuses it, from every entry point.</strong> One funnel: a <code>BodyRef</code> (sun, planet, moon, small body, belt) reaches one <code>selectBody</code>, which opens the panel (the body panels are a mutex), holds the one selection, draws the ring, and flies the camera (600 ms, ease-out). The canvas, the INDEX row, a resident, a belt member, <code>?id=</code> and a panel link all end there.</li><li><strong>Camera frames.</strong> A planet or a moon lands at 8 radii (zoom 1.5 to 50), a small body at 14 (4 to 600 units), a belt frames the whole ring from the Sun (570 for the asteroid belt, 1,430 for the Kuiper belt). The Sun, and a ref with no scene object, fly to the heliocentric home view.</li><li><strong>A ring for every kind that has a body</strong>: planets, moons, small bodies (3 radii), the Sun (1.25). A belt has no ring; its particles brighten (opacity x1.8, size x1.3) while it is selected. No new icon. <strong>The ring follows the selection, not the panel</strong>, for every kind: × closes the panel and the body stays selected, ringed and centred until another selection, Reset View or its chip turned off clears it.</li><li><strong>Pluto is one body with one panel</strong>, the small-body panel, on the canvas, in the INDEX, from a resident and from <code>?id=</code>; the scene draws it as a planet-style mesh, which takes the ring and the focus.</li><li><strong>Chips own kinds.</strong> Asteroids and Arrokoth ride DWARFS in 2D and 3D (the DWARFS tooltip names them, in 14 locales); ISM hides only ʻOumuamua. A chip turned <strong>off clears a selection of its kind</strong>: panel, ring and focus (the camera flies to the home view whenever a selection is cleared, whichever body held the focus). Selecting a body whose chip is off (a row, a resident, a link, <code>?id=</code>) <strong>turns the chip on</strong>. PATHS off closes a mission opened from a path. The same rule on Worlds: ORBITERS clears a selected orbiter; TRAVERSES has no selection of its own (its trail draws for the selected rover, which the chip does not hide), so it drops the stop tooltip only.</li><li><strong>No object, no link.</strong> A resident is a button only when the scene has an object for it: Pallas, Hygiea, Quaoar and Sedna are plain text until they have data (a content decision, not this slice&#39;s). The Belt members are links under the same rule; Vesta is one.</li><li><strong>Mission links open in place.</strong> A mission id in a body panel&#39;s Missions tab opens the mission panel over the body panel (the body stays selected). Only the mission is painted: the body panel under it stays mounted, covered (not painted, not focusable, Escape leaves it), so its tab and scroll come back, and the panel framing counts the one drawer. The mission panel&#39;s bar carries the back chip (<code>panel-back</code>), which returns to the body panel; × closes the mission panel, which is the same return, as the body panel is the one under it. A fleet id still navigates to <code>/fleet?id=</code>. Selecting another body closes a mission opened this way. The lists are <strong>newest first</strong>, sorted in code from the first year of each entry (the permutation is the same in all 14 locales, tested), the data untouched.</li><li><strong>One panel for all 18 iconic rows.</strong> The five outbound craft (Voyager 1 and 2, Pioneer 10 and 11, New Horizons) open the mission panel like the rest; their message (status, heading, signal reach, the culture door) is a section of its Overview. <code>MessagePanel</code> is deleted. A milestone marker, a launch marker or a milestone box on a trajectory opens its mission (the <code>userData</code> contract is <code>PICK_KINDS</code>, tested, and a re-skin keeps it); a box ranks below a real body and above a pick-aid. A belt is an area, not a body: a marker or box drawn over the belt takes the click, and the belt keeps it only where no marker is hit (2026-11-13, found on a phone, where the inner path markers lie over the belt).</li><li><strong>Outer scales.</strong> A member from the canvas or an INDEX row pans the camera to it and eases the zoom to 0.6 x the scale&#39;s entry distance (never below the floor, never out from a closer view the user chose). All eight scales have a Reset; the four that lacked one (Local Sheet, Virgo, Laniakea, Cosmic web) gain it. (It appeared once something was selected; <a href="./UXD-021-explore-hud-scale-bar-picker-pills-references">UXD-021</a> draws it always, disabled while there is nothing to reset, so the row does not shift. &quot;Nothing to reset&quot; means the view is at the entry pose: × on a member&#39;s panel keeps the camera on the member, so Reset stays live after it (2026-11-13, found on a phone, where × is the way past the sheet). Reset returns to the entry pose of the window&#39;s own framing, the portrait one on a portrait phone.) A member is a link: <code>?context=&lt;scale&gt;&amp;pick=&lt;id&gt;</code> for the five scales without a URL parameter of their own, mirrored both ways. Cygnus X-1 gets a Milky Way pin placed from its ra, dec and distance. A black hole row, or a canvas pick of such a pin, takes the camera to the hole&#39;s pin first and opens the lensing view from there, so leaving it lands on the pin. M87* has no pin of its own: M87 is the central galaxy of the Virgo Cluster, so selecting M87* selects the Virgo Cluster pin (camera, highlight, panel) and then opens the lensing view. Cygnus X-1 does the same with its Milky Way pin (so does Sagittarius A*). Gargantua has no pin: its panel opens alone. Until <a href="./../../wip/2026-11-12-slice-B3-brief">B3</a> makes the lensing view a button on the hole&#39;s panel, that chain is how a hole row gets there.</li><li><strong>Panel-aware framing.</strong> With a side drawer open the look-at point sits in the middle of the space it leaves (the projection shifts by half the drawer&#39;s width); a bottom sheet moves nothing. One helper (<code>panel-framing.ts</code>), shared with the Worlds&#39; orbiter framing (D1).</li><li><strong>L35.</strong> The mission panel&#39;s action row holds its place while the Mars or Moon surface site is read and shows its chips together, so Fly no longer shifts right and the hero no longer drops; a site list already read this session settles before the first paint. A mission that cannot fly (5 of the 52 Mars and Moon missions, 1 of them with a site) has no chip if the list turns up nothing, so the row would leave and everything below it move up: until the list is read that part is laid out and not painted, and the move happens unseen. The overview&#39;s message section of the five outbound craft holds its place the same way (its static text, a block for the culture door) while the trajectory loads. Amended 2026-11-15: the held place is two chip rows tall for a mission that can fly (the surface chip, then Fly and Share, wrap at the panel&#39;s 400 px), one row for the rest; with the one-row reservation Apollo 11&#39;s hero dropped 36 px when the site list arrived. <code>mission-panel-action-row.spec.ts</code> holds the site list 1.5 s and asserts the hero stays put under the row and Fly does not move.</li><li><strong>Plan mission on small bodies.</strong> Pluto moved from the planet panel to the small-body panel and lost its Plan mission chip; the small-body panel now carries it, deep-linked to <code>/plan?dest=&lt;id&gt;</code>, for the bodies /plan lists (Ceres, Vesta, Psyche, Bennu, Pluto; <code>src/lib/plan/destinations.ts</code>, shared with the /plan selector).</li></ol><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every body is on screen, ringed and centred when its panel opens. Turning a chip off while a body of its kind is selected loses the panel (re-select from the INDEX). The camera now moves on every INDEX row. <code>MissionPanel</code> gains the message section for five ids. The 2D view draws no ring (the 2D drawing code was not read; the selection is held, the panel opens). Pluto, now the small-body panel everywhere, no longer opens the planet panel&#39;s TacticalScan and gravity overlays (they gate on a selected planet, and the small-body path does not set one); it keeps the ring, the focus and, now, the Plan mission chip. Not covered here: Pallas, Hygiea, Quaoar and Sedna as data, the ruler&#39;s &quot;firsts&quot; links (C5), the hover ring and the label declutter (C2), a black hole as a pin selection (B3), Worlds&#39; camera (D1).</p><h2 id="verification" tabindex="-1">Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>tests/e2e/select-focus.spec.ts</code>, <code>scale-index.spec.ts</code> (a row also takes the camera), <code>explore-message-panel.spec.ts</code>, the unit tests <code>body-ref.test.ts</code>, <code>panel-framing.test.ts</code>, <code>missions-linkify.sort.test.ts</code>, <code>iconic-trajectory.test.ts</code>, <code>milky-way-visual.test.ts</code>, and the slice&#39;s check script (<code>docs/wip/2026-11-12-slice-C1-brief.md</code> §6).</p>',10)])])}const u=o(n,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
